      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                                            FAULT TOLERANCE\nThe Mental Model:\n\nFailure is not an exception. It is the norm.\n\nIn a large distributed system:\n\n- A machine fails approximately once every 1-3 years\n- With 1,000 machines, you expect a failure every 8-26 hours\n- With 100,000 machines (Google-scale), a failure happens every few minutes\n\nFault tolerance is not about preventing failures. Failures cannot be prevented. It’s about designing systems that continue operating correctly \ndespite failures.\n\n                                                                              TYPES OF FAILURES\n\n- Crash failures: A node stops completely. Easy to detect (no heartbeat), clear recovery path (failover to replica).\n\n- Byzantine failures: A node behaves incorrectly but doesn’t crash — it sends corrupted data, responds to some requests but not others, or re-\nturns different values to different callers. Much harder to handle. Requires Byzantine Fault Tolerant (BFT) algorithms (used in blockchain, aero-\nspace). Most distributed systems assume non-Byzantine failures.\n\n- Network failures: Messages are dropped, delayed, duplicated, or reordered. Partial connectivity (A can talk to B, B can talk to C, but A can’t \ntalk to C). This is the most common failure type in production.\n\n- Slowness: A node is alive but responding very slowly. From the outside, indistinguishable from crash. A timeout that’s too short causes you to \ntreat slow nodes as failed; too long and you wait forever for a dead node.\n\n                                                                    FAULT TOLERANCE STRATEGIES\n1) Replication\n\nStore multiple copies. If one replica fails, others serve the data. Covered in depth above.\n\n2) Health Checks and Automatic Removal\n\njavascript:\n\n// Load balancer health check implementation\nconst checkHealth = async (instance) => {\n    try {\n        const response = await fetch(`http://${instance.host}/health`, {\n            signal: AbortSignal.timeout(2000) // 2 second timeout\n        });\n\n        if (response.ok) {\n            const body = await response.json();\n            // Deep health check: verify dependencies too\n            return body.status === 'healthy' && body.dependencies.database === 'ok';\n        }\n        return false;\n    } catch {\n        return false; // timeout or connection refused = unhealthy\n    }\n};\n\n// Check every 10 seconds, remove instance after 2 consecutive failures\nconst healthChecker = setInterval(async () => {\n    for (const instance of instances) {\n        const healthy = await checkHealth(instance);\n        if (!healthy) {\n            instance.failCount++;\n            if (instance.failCount >= 2) {\n                removeFromRotation(instance);\n                alert(`Instance ${instance.host} removed from rotation`);\n            }\n        } else {\n            instance.failCount = 0;\n        }\n    }\n}, 10000);\n\n\n3) Graceful Degradation\n\nWhen a non-critical component fails, the system continues working with reduced functionality rather than failing completely.\n\nReal example — Netflix:\n\nPrimary: Personalised recommendations from ML service\nIf ML service is down: Show popular titles in your region (degraded)\nIf region data is down: Show globally popular titles (more degraded)\nIf everything is down: Show any content (not curated, but watchable)\n\nThe system never shows a blank screen just because recommendations are unavailable. The content streaming continues regardless.\n\n4) Implementation using feature flags:\n\njavascript:\n\nconst getRecommendations = async (userId) => {\n    try {\n        // Try the personalized ML service\n        const recs = await mlService.getPersonalized(userId);\n        return recs;\n    } catch {\n        try {\n            // Fallback: regional popular content\n            return await contentService.getRegionalPopular(userId);\n        } catch {\n            // Final fallback: globally popular\n            return await contentService.getGlobalPopular();\n        }\n    }\n};\n\n5) Bulkheads\n\nThe term comes from ship design — ships have bulkhead walls that prevent flooding in one section from sinking the whole ship. In software, bulk-\nheads isolate failures between components.\n\njavascript:\n\n// Without bulkheads: one slow service blocks all threads\n// The Payment Service is slow → all threads wait for it → no threads left for Users Service\n\n// With bulkheads: separate thread pools per downstream service\nconst userServicePool = new ThreadPool({ maxThreads: 20 });\nconst paymentServicePool = new ThreadPool({ maxThreads: 10 });\nconst inventoryServicePool = new ThreadPool({ maxThreads: 15 });\n\n// Payment Service being slow only exhausts the payment pool\n// User Service requests still get threads from their pool\n\nKubernetes resource limits are a form of bulkheads — one pod can’t consume all CPU/memory on a node, protecting other pods.\n\nChaos Engineering\n\nNetflix invented Chaos Monkey — a tool that randomly terminates production instances to force engineers to build genuinely fault-tolerant \nsystems.\n\nChaos Monkey randomly picks a production service instance and terminates it.\nIf the system handles it gracefully → fault tolerance is working.\nIf the system breaks → discover the fault in a controlled way, not at 3am during peak traffic.\n\nThe philosophy: You cannot test fault tolerance in a staging environment that doesn’t replicate production load and complexity. You must test \nin production, but in a controlled way.\n\nChaos engineering has evolved to Chaos Gorilla (entire availability zones), Chaos Kong (entire AWS regions), and Latency Monkey (inject network \ndelays).\n\nThe Error Budget — Google’s SRE Approach\n\nGoogle’s Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practice introduced the concept of an error budget:\n\nSLA: 99.9% availability (the \"three nines\")\nError budget: 100% - 99.9% = 0.1% downtime allowed\n\n0.1% of a year = 8.76 hours/year of acceptable downtime\n0.1% of a month = 43.8 minutes/month of acceptable downtime\n\nThe error budget is a quantified allowance for failure. When you’re within budget, you can take risks (deploy new features, make infrastructure \nchanges). When you’ve exhausted the budget, you freeze changes and focus entirely on reliability.\n\nThis turns reliability from a vague goal (“we want high availability”) into a concrete engineering constraint (“we have 43 minutes of downtime bud-\nget this month, we’ve used 15, we have 28 left”).",
